Z||000000||Black88GT||06-19-2001||04:24 PM||||Well its not a total shock if you live in Baltimore since there has been some talk about it, but I guess it is a little surprising.  Cal is the biggest icon around here.  I remember sneaking in to the game where he broke the unbreakable record, it was magical.  He will be missed, don't see too many players like him anymore.  Take A.rod for example, leaves the Mariners for money.  Ripken spent his entire career with the Orioles, even minor league.  Over 3,000 hits and over 400 homeruns, 18 time all star, and of course the streak.  What more can I say.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">     <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||1
Z||000001||Galen||06-19-2001||05:20 PM||||Thats because players dont play for the game anymore, they play for the money. Players arn't loyal to teams anymore. And those who are, are a dying breed. People ask whats wrong with sports now a days? that Tom Cruise movie said why "Show me the money"<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/angryboy.gif">      <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>   -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||06-20-2001||01:02 AM||||Coming from a Seattle native...who cares about ARod and his money?  Could you turn down an extra 100 million bucks?????!!!!  I didn't thinks so.  Oh, by the way, which would be more important to fans today: a) having the best season we've ever had with fresh talent and kicking everybody's ass or b) having several of the "top notch" players we lost over the last couple years (Randy Johnson, Griffey Jr., A. Rodriguez, etc.)<P>Obviously, he went for the money, but when you're talking a double in pay, who could turn it down?  I am far from a fairweather fan, so I'm loving that the Mariners are finally doing good.<P>I loved Ripken, only got to see him play once, but he is a great role model.  I'm glad to see him retire, he deserves it.<P>Yes, it's great to have loyal players, but in some cases, it's nice to have a winning team now and then rather than a mediocre season with loyal players.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||06-20-2001||01:28 AM||||Bryce, I can understand wanting more money, it's human nature.  But there is something to be said for people who will stay loyal to one city.  Ripken grew up in Aberdeen which is about 30 min. from downtown Baltimore, about 15 from my house.  He now lives in Reisterstown which is also about 15 min. from my house.  His Dad coached here, he and his brother played here, its just not something you see everyday.  Usually they sell out to higher paying teams, move their families across country, and never look back.  Cal is starting a 30 million dollar little league baseball project in his hometown of Aberdeen.  He already helped build a minor league stadium and his Dad started a scholarship program there and a baseball camp.<P>I totally agree with you in Arods case.  I am pretty sure he didn't grow up in or around Seattle.  He is young and wanted more money, OK thats fine (not really double in pay though, more like a long term contract).  It's great whats going on with Seattle, but I mean, you guys would still be awesome with Arod, Griffey and/or Johnson you have to admit.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||06-20-2001||02:41 AM||||Yeah, like I said, I think Ripken is a great role model.  My dad coached a Babe Ruth baseball team, and Ripken is starting to get really involved with that organization.  They started a Cal Ripken league, so his name has joined those of Sandy Kofax, Mickey Mantle, and Babe Ruth in the organization.<P>As far as those big shot players.  We had them, remember?  Especially those three guys, we had them all at once, but I don't seem to recall any absolutely amazing seasons before.  I think a large part of our success is that we are a team now, not four awesome players and some other guys.  Another big part of it is pitching staff.  This is the first time in history the Mariners have a had a consistently excellent pitching staff.  We've had great bats for a long time, but some solid pitching and fielders has made a huge difference.  Obviously, we could speculate all day, this is just what I think is up.<P>Oh, and by the way, anybody interested in buying an Arod autographed baseball?  I got it personally autographed a few years ago...that was pretty cool...I touched Arod <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero Sam||06-20-2001||06:18 PM||||I am from Maryland originally and I follow the Orioles on Direct TV.I am boycotting the y$$$$$$.I won't watch them.My family won't even say the word.I guess Cal's last game will be against them so I will have to watch that.<BR>To Nashco:You should be excited about the Mariners season so far.I bet a couple of those guys who left are wishing they were still there(even if they won't admit it).<BR>I will be excited for you and your team if they will just knock off the y$$$$$$ in the playoffs.||||reg||1
